    {
      "id": "0b8cb383-f965-4883-9541-0f6be1d9308a",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# LinkedHashSet – properties and methods\r\n\r\n`LinkedHashSet` is a `HashSet` that **remembers the insertion order**.\r\n\r\n**Properties**\r\n- No duplicates, allows one `null`.\r\n- Items come out in the **order you added them**.\r\n- Almost as fast as `HashSet` (uses a little more memory to remember the order).\r\n- Since **Java 21** it also has methods to work with both ends: `getFirst`, `getLast`, `addFirst`, `addLast`, `removeFirst`, `removeLast`, `reversed`.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "0b8cb383-f965-4883-9541-0f6be1d9308a"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1a3ab35e-4abf-4ba5-9777-75ec5d643aa5",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//Syntax\r\n//Set<DataType> variable_name = new LinkedHashSet<DataType>();\r\n\r\n//this is to show the order difference between HashSet and LinkedHashSet\r\n// Method: new LinkedHashSet<>()\r\n// What:   creates an empty set that keeps the insertion order\r\n// Use:    when you need unique values AND the order you added them\r\nSet<String> linked = new LinkedHashSet<String>();\r\nSet<String> hash = new HashSet<String>();\r\n\r\nfor (String city : List.of(\"Pune\", \"Mumbai\", \"Goa\", \"Delhi\", \"Pune\")) {\r\n    // Method: add(item)\r\n    // What:   adds the item if it is not already there\r\n    // Use:    to fill both sets with the same values\r\n    linked.add(city);\r\n    hash.add(city);\r\n}\r\n\r\nSystem.out.println(\"LinkedHashSet: \" + linked);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"HashSet:       \" + hash);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "1a3ab35e-4abf-4ba5-9777-75ec5d643aa5",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 15,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 15,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "LinkedHashSet: [Pune, Mumbai, Goa, Delhi]\r\nHashSet:       [Delhi, Goa, Pune, Mumbai]\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 15
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "f8d028ea-f6e5-43d1-846c-59ba46915853",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "LinkedHashSet<String> cities = new LinkedHashSet<>(List.of(\"Mumbai\", \"Delhi\", \"Pune\"));\r\n\r\n//Java 21+: methods to work with the first and last items\r\n// Method: getFirst() / getLast()   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   returns the first / last item\r\n// Use:    to read the ends without a loop\r\nSystem.out.println(\"First: \" + cities.getFirst() + \", Last: \" + cities.getLast());\r\n\r\n// Method: addFirst(item) / addLast(item)   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   adds the item at the start / end (if it is already there, it is moved)\r\n// Use:    to control where a value goes\r\ncities.addFirst(\"Goa\");\r\ncities.addLast(\"Jaipur\");\r\nSystem.out.println(\"After addFirst/addLast: \" + cities);\r\n\r\n// Method: removeFirst() / removeLast()   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   removes and returns the first / last item\r\n// Use:    to take values from either end\r\ncities.removeFirst();\r\ncities.removeLast();\r\nSystem.out.println(\"After removeFirst/removeLast: \" + cities);\r\n\r\n// Method: reversed()   [Java 21+]\r\n// What:   returns a reversed view of the set\r\n// Use:    to read the set from last to first\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Reversed: \" + cities.reversed());",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "f8d028ea-f6e5-43d1-846c-59ba46915853",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 16,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 16,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "First: Mumbai, Last: Pune\r\nAfter addFirst/addLast: [Goa, Mumbai, Delhi, Pune, Jaipur]\r\nAfter removeFirst/removeLast: [Mumbai, Delhi, Pune]\r\nReversed: [Pune, Delhi, Mumbai]\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 16
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b1e6e1e6-bc21-46cd-b1c9-f9ae2ad806bc",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# TreeSet – properties and methods\r\n\r\n`TreeSet` keeps its values **sorted** (numbers small → big, text A → Z).\r\n\r\n**Properties**\r\n- No duplicates, **no `null`** (it throws an error, because `null` cannot be compared).\r\n- Always sorted, in natural order or by a `Comparator` you give it.\r\n- Slower than `HashSet` (`add`, `remove`, `contains` take `O(log n)`), but gives you extra methods to search by order: `first`, `last`, `floor`, `ceiling`, `headSet`, `tailSet`, …\r\n- Declare it as `TreeSet<...>` (or `NavigableSet<...>`) to use those extra methods; a plain `Set<...>` variable does not have them.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "b1e6e1e6-bc21-46cd-b1c9-f9ae2ad806bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c1f12004-0cab-491e-b53f-e46c19c5f037",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//Syntax\r\n//TreeSet<DataType> variable_name = new TreeSet<DataType>();\r\n\r\n//this is to create a sorted set\r\n// Method: new TreeSet<>()\r\n// What:   creates an empty set that keeps its values sorted\r\n// Use:    when you need unique values in sorted order\r\nTreeSet<Integer> marks = new TreeSet<Integer>();\r\nmarks.add(70);\r\nmarks.add(45);\r\nmarks.add(90);\r\nmarks.add(60);\r\nmarks.add(45); // duplicate, not added\r\nSystem.out.println(\"The set is: \" + marks);\r\n\r\n// Method: first() / last()\r\n// What:   returns the smallest / biggest item\r\n// Use:    to get the minimum and maximum quickly\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Lowest: \" + marks.first() + \", Highest: \" + marks.last());",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "c1f12004-0cab-491e-b53f-e46c19c5f037",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 17,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "The set is: [45, 60, 70, 90]\r\nLowest: 45, Highest: 90\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 17
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5dec11fa-86bb-49ff-8ba9-45a1d0a6cfa2",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "TreeSet<Integer> marks = new TreeSet<>(List.of(45, 60, 70, 90));\r\n\r\n//this is to find the closest values to a number\r\n// Method: floor(x) / ceiling(x)\r\n// What:   biggest item <= x / smallest item >= x (null if none)\r\n// Use:    to find the nearest value, including x itself\r\nSystem.out.println(\"floor(65): \" + marks.floor(65));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"ceiling(65): \" + marks.ceiling(65));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"floor(70): \" + marks.floor(70));\r\n\r\n// Method: lower(x) / higher(x)\r\n// What:   biggest item < x / smallest item > x (x itself is never returned)\r\n// Use:    to find the value just before / just after\r\nSystem.out.println(\"lower(70): \" + marks.lower(70));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"higher(70): \" + marks.higher(70));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"higher(90): \" + marks.higher(90));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "5dec11fa-86bb-49ff-8ba9-45a1d0a6cfa2",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 18,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 18,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "floor(65): 60\r\nceiling(65): 70\r\nfloor(70): 70\r\nlower(70): 60\r\nhigher(70): 90\r\nhigher(90): null\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 18
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dcd9817f-5060-4d24-b092-b365bd61b4f6",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "TreeSet<Integer> marks = new TreeSet<>(List.of(45, 60, 70, 90, 100));\r\n\r\n//this is to get a part of the set\r\n// Method: headSet(to)\r\n// What:   items smaller than \"to\" (to is excluded)\r\n// Use:    to get everything below a value\r\nSystem.out.println(\"headSet(70): \" + marks.headSet(70));\r\n\r\n// Method: tailSet(from)\r\n// What:   items from \"from\" upwards (from is included)\r\n// Use:    to get everything from a value up\r\nSystem.out.println(\"tailSet(70): \" + marks.tailSet(70));\r\n\r\n// Method: subSet(from, to)\r\n// What:   items from \"from\" (included) to \"to\" (excluded)\r\n// Use:    to get a range of values\r\nSystem.out.println(\"subSet(60, 100): \" + marks.subSet(60, 100));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "dcd9817f-5060-4d24-b092-b365bd61b4f6",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 19,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 19,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "headSet(70): [45, 60]\r\ntailSet(70): [70, 90, 100]\r\nsubSet(60, 100): [60, 70, 90]\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 19
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6e454e4d-7517-4b3e-92b5-e53ae8e69d52",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "TreeSet<Integer> marks = new TreeSet<>(List.of(45, 60, 70, 90));\r\n\r\n//this is to read the set from biggest to smallest\r\n// Method: descendingSet()\r\n// What:   returns a reversed (big → small) view of the set\r\n// Use:    to show values in descending order\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Descending: \" + marks.descendingSet());\r\n\r\n//this is to take out the smallest and the biggest item\r\n// Method: pollFirst() / pollLast()\r\n// What:   removes and returns the smallest / biggest item (null if the set is empty)\r\n// Use:    to process values in order, one at a time\r\nSystem.out.println(\"pollFirst: \" + marks.pollFirst());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"pollLast: \" + marks.pollLast());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"The set is: \" + marks);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "6e454e4d-7517-4b3e-92b5-e53ae8e69d52",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 20,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 20,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "Descending: [90, 70, 60, 45]\r\npollFirst: 45\r\npollLast: 90\r\nThe set is: [60, 70]\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 20
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ebde4ff2-2f1a-4233-8f2b-6e9e61bace2e",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//this is to sort in a different order with a Comparator\r\n// Method: new TreeSet<>(Comparator.reverseOrder())\r\n// What:   creates a TreeSet that sorts big → small (Z → A for text)\r\n// Use:    when the natural order is not the one you need\r\nTreeSet<String> names = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());\r\nnames.addAll(List.of(\"Zoya\", \"Jane\", \"Doe\", \"Arbaz\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Z to A: \" + names);\r\n\r\n// Method: new TreeSet<>(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER)\r\n// What:   sorts text ignoring upper/lower case (\"apple\" and \"Apple\" count as the same item)\r\n// Use:    for case-insensitive unique words\r\nTreeSet<String> words = new TreeSet<>(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER);\r\nwords.addAll(List.of(\"banana\", \"Apple\", \"apple\", \"Cherry\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Case-insensitive: \" + words);\r\n\r\n//names.add(null);   // not allowed: TreeSet cannot store null (the program stops with an error)",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "ebde4ff2-2f1a-4233-8f2b-6e9e61bace2e",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 21,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "Z to A: [Zoya, Jane, Doe, Arbaz]\r\nCase-insensitive: [Apple, banana, Cherry]\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 21
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1be433e9-551b-41c6-9098-ed2c7dc7ad02",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# HashSet vs LinkedHashSet vs TreeSet\r\n\r\n| | `HashSet` | `LinkedHashSet` | `TreeSet` |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Order | None (any order) | Insertion order | Sorted |\r\n| Duplicates | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |\r\n| `null` | ✅ one | ✅ one | ❌ |\r\n| Speed of `add` / `remove` / `contains` | Fastest `O(1)` | Fast `O(1)` | Slower `O(log n)` |\r\n| Extra methods | – | `getFirst`, `addFirst`, `reversed`… (Java 21+) | `first`, `last`, `floor`, `ceiling`, `headSet`, `tailSet`, `pollFirst`… |\r\n| Use when | Only uniqueness matters | You need uniqueness + the order you added | You need uniqueness + sorted values |\r\n\r\n**Set vs List**\r\n\r\n| | `List` (`ArrayList`) | `Set` (`HashSet`) |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Duplicates | ✅ allowed | ❌ not allowed |\r\n| Index (`get(i)`) | ✅ | ❌ |\r\n| `contains` speed | Slow (checks every item) | Very fast |",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "1be433e9-551b-41c6-9098-ed2c7dc7ad02"
      }
    }
  ]
}